{
  "summary": "Configure Guix release builds for Linux x86_64 and aarch64 to produce non-GUI binaries (bitcoind and CLI tools) as static position-independent executables (-static-pie) using glibc 2.44. Apply patches to glibc to eliminate non-deterministic store paths and avoid dlopen in NSS modules, while mapping store paths with compiler prefix flags. Update symbol and security checks to validate static ELFs without dynamic interpreters.",
  "problem": "Linux release binaries dynamically link against glibc, preventing them from running on non-glibc distributions such as Alpine or on systems with glibc versions older than the build toolchain's. Dynamic linking also complicates security hardening features such as pointer authentication on aarch64 (#19075).",
  "evidence": [
    "PR description: 'Using glibc for static builds, would be less of a leap compared to switching to musl libc (POC in #23203).'",
    "Release notes: 'Release binaries for x86_64 and aarch64 Linux (except for bitcoin-qt) are now compiled with -static-pie, making them more portable. They can now be run on non-glibc based systems, as well as on systems than ship with older glibcs, than were previously supported.'"
  ],
